{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdyCh9W1rjikbDvkb27WL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Audit Conclusion: Golden-Snapshot Gate (T6S2)\n\n## Assessment\n\n1. **Current Design: Acceptable as-is**\n   - The gate's design aligns with documented, intentional policy (ADR-007). The constraint is not accidental or overlooked—it is *deliberate* and ratified.\n   - Full-precision serialization of iterative solver outputs is inherently runtime-fragile (floating-point semantics vary across Node majors). This is a known trade-off, not a hidden risk.\n\n2. **What Must Change: Nothing operationally; clarify governance**\n   - No code or CI changes are required if payroll reproducibility under Node 20 is still the binding requirement.\n   - Document the colleague's concern as a *design debt item* linked to ADR-007's sunset clause (\"revisit at Node 20 EOL\").\n   - Explicitly record that the gate **intentionally does not** guarantee cross-major-version reproducibility.\n\n3. **Second-Major Proof: Not required under current policy**\n   - The ADR explicitly rejects runtime currency in favor of bitwise reproducibility *within* Node 20. A second major would violate that principle.\n   - If organizational risk tolerance has changed (e.g., Node 20 EOL looms), the ADR should be *revised* before the gate is modified—not the gate justified by new criteria retroactively.\n\n4. **Recommendation**\n   - Confirm with stakeholders (Finance, Compliance, DevOps) that Node 20 lock-in remains acceptable through [Node 20 EOL date: April 2026].\n   - If yes: close the flag as \"design-as-intended\"; schedule ADR review 6 months before EOL.\n   - If no: revise ADR-007 to permit tolerance bands or snapshot rounding, then gate on a second major *as part of the new policy*.\n\n**Current gate status: Green and defensible.** The flag surfaced valid fragility, but not a defect—a known constraint needing clearer ownership."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","